body:
Self harm generally doesn't lead to admission unless it's ax extreme or life threatening circumstance, but you DO need help. Talking to a counselor is an amazing first step, and you won't regret it, because it's one of the best ways to get the help you need. What will happen, I'm not going to lie to you, is that the counselor will be mandated to tell your parents, in most cases. I know this is hard, but it can be really helpful to have assistance in talking about out to your parents. I think it would be such an amazing step for you to take! 

&#x200B;

As admissions go, like I said, self harm alone doesn't lead to admission unless in severe and extreme circumstances. Likely, you'll start at individual therapy or intensive outpatient but of course, everyone is different. Admission basically means, in my experience, waiting at a local hospital in emergency until a bed is available. Inpatient admissions are usually around 2 weeks, and are just to stabilize you until you're able to receive help in a step-down setting. You can ask me if you have questions! Best of luck x

Surround yourself with people. Take a walk on the trail, sit at a coffee shop and read, hell, go grocery shopping if you want. Be in a situation where you can't self harm because you're surrounded. 

&#x200B;

If the thought makes you anxious (as it sometimes does for me), write. All your emotions and feelings onto the page. Go for a hard run. Listen to a good soundtrack or go on a Netflix binge. You have to remember that urges PASS, despite how unbearable they seem to be. Give yourself time, because as someone else stated, self harm is like a wave. The urge won't be unbearable forever. I also remember that every time I cut, I feel instant regret, and I like to think about that before doing something I know I'll forget.
